<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tudhaliya IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1237–1209 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tudhaliya IV was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> in the thirteenth century BCE, one of its last significant rulers. His reign saw extensive religious reorganisation and building, but also mounting pressures on the empire. Accounts of the Hittite Empire list succession conflicts after his death among the factors, along with drought, famine and the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> network, that contributed to the empire's collapse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv.jpg" alt="Tudhaliya IV" width="289" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: China_Crisis · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yazilikaya.TudhaliyaIV.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other factors were succession conflicts around the reign of <b>Tudhaliya IV</b>, prolonged drought and famine documented in correspondence requesting emergency grain shipments from Egypt, and the fragmentation of the vassal network that had sustained imperial revenues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In a treaty of <b>Tudhaliya IV</b> the king of Ahhiyawa was written among the great kings of the age and then struck out.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In a treaty of <b>Tudhaliya IV</b>, c. 1230 BCE, the king of Ahhiyawa was struck from the list of great kings, suggesting its power had waned.</blockquote><a href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a></li><li><blockquote>The so-called Milawata letter, probably from <b>Tudhaliya IV</b>, shows the town had passed under Hittite control and a new border was being fixed.</blockquote><a href="/state/millawanda">Millawanda</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Tudhaliya IV</b> a king backed by Ahhiyawa rebelled and was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/state/seha-river-land">Seha River Land</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty between <b>Tudhaliya IV</b> and Kurunta, on a bronze tablet found at Hattusa in 1986, defines its borders and privileges.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarhuntassa">Tarhuntassa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerik">Nerik</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.21,35.42&amp;year=-1223" aria-label="Nerik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5415 694 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tudhaliya IV"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5865 964h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5865 964h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.21,35.42&amp;year=-1223">What was here in 1223 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
